This would be trouble. IF you do that instead of
PATH=%PATH%;c:\mingw\bin you loose any external commands from windows
being used. i.e. xcopy, move deltree and such. I would try the second
way. As it won't do any harm and it might change how the compile is run.
The errors you get might be a side effect of this.In fact in the
sys\winnt\install.nt - which you should use if you have problems (open
it with wordpad - it uses linefeeds instead of carridge returns) it
says: For the
    GCC Makefile, add <mingw>\bin to your path, where <mingw> is your
    MinGW root directory.)
Which is what that does.



see:

http://www.microsoft.com/resources/documentation/windows/xp/all/proddocs/
en-us/path.mspx?mfr=true 

if you're curious. BTW you can get the options for any dos command this
way - this is what microsoft replaced the dos manuals with - and added
any problems/fixes needed with any microsoft packages. 


You did get: nethack-343-src.tgz  and saved it *with* the same name
right? Did you extract it with full paths to c:\nh343\src. The compiler
you want is the one that *just* is called MinGW which downloads
MinGW-5.1.1.exe currently. You wanted the g++ compiler, the MinGW base
tools and MinGW make. Only the first is checked by default. You may need
disable your firewall (if you have one - recommended) to allow it - I
had to. Choose the download *and* install, otherwise you will make too
much work for yourself. 

With the nethack source, make sure that you *don't* extract the
directory nethack.3.4.3, but instead extract everything *in* that
directory to c:\nh343 with paths. 



The same line above should be used for the path= line in install.bat.

